# Surgery or medication?
subtitle: "How a decision tree weighs every outcome by its chance to pick between surgery and tablets, and how safe that choice really is."
voices: {"a":"gemini:Gacrux | a mature, calm clinician thinking aloud; measured and kind","take":"lines"}

## Surgery or medication?
use: decision_tree
with:
    root:
      id: choice
      type: decision
      label: Treatment
      children: [{label: '', node: {id: surgery, type: chance, label: Surgery, children: [{label: Works, probability: 0.85, node: {id: s_ok, type: terminal, label: Cured}}, {label: Complication, probability: 0.15, node: {id: s_bad, type: terminal, label: Harmed}}], value: ''}}, {label: '', node: {id: med, type: chance, label: Medication, children: [{label: Controlled, probability: 0.7, node: {id: m_ok, type: terminal, label: Well}}, {label: Not controlled, probability: 0.3, node: {id: m_bad, type: terminal, label: Still ill}}], value: ''}}]
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 215
      w: 900
      h: 470

    math threshold tex "9(1-p) + 3p = 7.4 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; p \\approx 0.27" size 30 x 500 y 140 hidden true
    text q_s_ok "9" #3d3833 x 905 y 650 font_size 34 hidden true
    text q_s_bad "3" #3d3833 x 905 y 540 font_size 34 hidden true
    text q_m_ok "8" #3d3833 x 905 y 409 font_size 34 hidden true
    text q_m_bad "6" #3d3833 x 905 y 299 font_size 34 hidden true
    text q_head "QALYs" #5a534c x 905 y 690 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Surgery or medication?"

Surgery or tablets? When both treatments are gambles, how does a decision tree choose? Here the same disease can be operated on or treated with medication.
    draw node_choice label_choice

The square is the choice we make. Choose surgery, and we reach a circle: from here on, chance decides.
    draw edge_choice_surgery node_surgery label_surgery

Eighty-five times in a hundred the operation works, and the patient can expect nine quality-adjusted life years.
    draw edge_surgery_s_ok branchlabel_surgery_s_ok node_s_ok label_s_ok q_s_ok q_head

Fifteen times in a hundred there is a serious complication, and only three QALYs.
    draw edge_surgery_s_bad branchlabel_surgery_s_bad node_s_bad label_s_bad q_s_bad

    pause 0.4

Medication is a gamble too, only a milder one.
    draw edge_choice_med node_med label_med

Seven in ten are well controlled, at eight QALYs; the other three in ten get six.
    draw edge_med_m_ok branchlabel_med_m_ok node_m_ok label_m_ok q_m_ok edge_med_m_bad branchlabel_med_m_bad node_m_bad label_m_bad q_m_bad

    pause 0.4

Compare the best cases and surgery wins, nine to eight.
    highlight q_s_ok q_m_ok

Compare the worst cases and medication wins, six to three. Neither comparison is the answer.
    highlight q_s_bad q_m_bad

Instead, fold the tree back: weight each ending by its chance. Nine times 0.85 plus three times 0.15 gives surgery 8.1 expected QALYs.
    math ev_s tex "9 \\times 0.85 + 3 \\times 0.15 = 8.1" x 440 y 455

Eight times 0.7 plus six times 0.3 gives medication 7.4.
    math ev_m tex "8 \\times 0.7 + 6 \\times 0.3 = 7.4" x 440 y 205

Surgery, by 0.7 QALYs a patient, even though about one patient in seven ends up worse off than on tablets.
    highlight ev_s label_surgery

    pause 0.4

How safe is that verdict? Let the complication risk be p. Surgery keeps winning until p passes about 0.27.
    draw threshold

So the question for the evidence is not the average risk, but whether it could plausibly be above one in four.
    highlight threshold part "0.27"

    quiz "The complication risk turns out to be 0.2, not 0.15. Which option does the tree now pick?"
        right: Still surgery: nine times 0.8 plus three times 0.2 is 7.8, above medication's 7.4. The switch comes only past about 0.27.
        + Surgery
        * Medication
        * It depends on the best case
---
